@* Principle 11 – Content appears and operates in predictable ways *@

<h2>Consistent navigation</h2>
<p>
    The header, navigation menu, and footer are in the same place, in the same order, on every page of this site.
    That's not luck: they all come from a single file, <code>Views/Shared/_Layout.cshtml</code>, and the menu is generated
    from one list, <code>Models/PrincipleCatalog.cs</code>. Using shared layouts and partial views makes consistency the default.
</p>

<h2>Consistent labels</h2>
<p>
    Components that do the same thing should have the same name everywhere. On this site the search button is always
    “Search” and the menu item for this page is always “Predictable behavior”. If one page said “Search”, another
    said “Find”, and a third used only a magnifying-glass icon, users would wonder whether they did different things.
</p>

<h2>No changes without consent</h2>
<p>
    Changing a form field, or moving focus onto something, should never by itself submit a form, open a new window,
    or move to a new page. Users choose when that happens, usually by pressing a button.
</p>
<div class="compare">
    <div class="example example-good">
        <h3>Do: wait for the user to press Go</h3>
        <form method="get" asp-action="Go">
            <div class="form-group">
                <label for="jump-good">Jump to a principle</label>
                <select id="jump-good" name="target">
                    @foreach (var p in PrincipleCatalog.All)
                    {
                        <option value="@p.Action">@p.ShortTitle</option>
                    }
                </select>
            </div>
            <button type="submit">Go</button>
        </form>
    </div>
    <div class="example example-bad">
        <h3>Don't: navigate when the selection changes (live example)</h3>
        <div class="form-group">
            <label for="jump-bad">Jump to a principle</label>
            <select id="jump-bad">
                <option value="">Choose…</option>
                @foreach (var p in PrincipleCatalog.All)
                {
                    <option value="@Url.Action(p.Action)">@p.ShortTitle</option>
                }
            </select>
        </div>
        <p>
            Focus this list with <kbd>Tab</kbd> and press the down arrow. In many browsers the page changes immediately,
            before a keyboard user can reach the option they wanted.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>
<code-sample kind="bad" label="Context change on input">
    <select onchange="location = this.value">…</select>
    <input onfocus="this.form.submit()">
</code-sample>

<h2>Warn before opening a new window</h2>
<p>
    Opening a new tab takes users out of their current context, and the Back button stops working.
    Avoid it, or say so in the link text:
</p>
<p>
    <a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">W3C Web Accessibility Initiative (opens in a new tab)</a>
</p>

<h2>Consistent help</h2>
<p>
    If a site offers help (a contact link, chat, or FAQ), it should be in the same relative place on every page.
    On this site the link back to the W3C source and the site map are always in the footer.
</p>

@section Scripts {
    <script src="~/js/predictable.js" asp-append-version="true"></script>
}
